title: 'pallet-scarcity: coinage-style NFT ownership with feeless rest-time-prioritized transfers'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Adds `pallet-scarcity`, a FRAME pallet for collectible instances under a
    coinage-style ownership model: one instance per fresh purse public key,
    with a stable `InstanceId` reverse index as the external handle. Item
    identity is immutable once written and may be minted many times. Metadata
    is a three-level mutable key/value store: collection entries provide
    defaults, item entries override the same key for every mint of that
    definition, and instance entries override both for one minted NFT. Only
    the collection owner may write these scopes; each entry has its own
    deposit and there is no metadata freeze mechanism.

    Storage deposits are paid on the supply side. The collection owner backs
    the collection record, item definitions, metadata entries, and ordinarily
    minted instances with one aggregate consideration ticket. The runtime
    chooses the consideration mechanism; for example, it may use a fungible
    hold, no consideration, or another runtime-defined policy. Collection
    ownership uses a two-step handoff: the current owner nominates a successor,
    then the successor claims by atomically establishing an equivalent ticket
    before the former owner's ticket is dropped. A failed claim leaves
    ownership and both tickets unchanged. Runtime pallets may use the
    `MintWithoutDeposit` integration trait to mint without an instance deposit
    when state growth is bounded by another scarce resource or protocol rule.
    Initial instance metadata is also depositless through this integration.

    The collection owner has full collection-scoped lifecycle control. It may
    force-transfer or force-burn individual instances, delete item definitions
    after their live instances and item metadata are removed, and delete a
    collection after its items and collection metadata are removed. Holders
    retain feeless transfer and burn operations. Cleanup is incremental and
    bounded, all deposits are released exactly, and collection, item, and
    instance identifiers are never reused.

    Transfers and burns are feeless and authorized by the owning purse key
    through the `AsScarcity` transaction extension, which elevates a custom
    `Origin::Nft` capability origin, consumes the instance in `prepare`,
    assigns transaction priority proportional to the instance's rest time
    (seconds since it last moved, capped), and restores the instance with an
    exponential backoff lock when dispatch fails. Burning releases instance
    and instance-metadata deposits while keeping minted-ever item supply
    monotonic and decrementing live supply. Authorizations bind to both the
    permanent instance identifier and its `state_nonce`. Every successful
    holder or collection-owner transfer increments `state_nonce`, so moving an
    instance away and back to the same purse cannot revive an earlier
    authorization. Following Coinage's purse model, NFT-authorized operations
    do not use the purse key's System account nonce. Consumption-on-use and a
    per-instance-state `provides` tag containing the encoded
    `(InstanceId, state_nonce)` tuple prevent concurrent use of the same
    authorization without a hash-collision risk. A failed dispatch restores
    the same NFT state behind an exponential backoff lock, so the same signed
    transaction can be submitted again after the lock expires.

    The pallet and `AsScarcity` extension are included in the development
    runtime for ordinary signed transactions, using timestamp time and balance
    holds for storage consideration. Extension ordering is security-critical:
    `AsScarcity` runs with the origin modifiers before `AuthorizeCall`,
    signed-account checks including `CheckNonce`, and transaction payment.
    Those account checks deliberately skip the resulting `Origin::Nft`, while
    payment observes it and skips feeless transfers and burns. NFT-only purse
    keys therefore require neither a System account nor a balance. This PR
    does not expose Scarcity calls to `pallet-contracts` or `pallet-revive`; a
    runtime-specific call filter, chain extension, or precompile remains
    necessary before a contract can act as a collection owner from contract
    code.

    A `try_state` hook verifies allocation bounds, dependency and metadata
    counters, live supply, collection/item references, the owner/reverse-index
    bijection, locks, metadata references, and each collection's accounted
    aggregate against its stored deposit components. Metadata growth is priced
    by a deposit per entry. `define_item` accepts a linearly weighted `Vec`
    whose batch size is bounded by extrinsic and block length, while each key
    and value remains individually bounded. Per-instance metadata has an
    explicit configured bound so burn cleanup remains bounded, and burn calls
    refund unused weight according to the number of entries actually removed.
crates:
- name: pallet-scarcity
  bump: major
- name: polkadot-sdk
  bump: minor
